    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d703">

                <pb facs="0685" n="677" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg677"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/2345: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Beirut</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-03-13T11:00:00+02:00">March 13, 1941—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 14—5:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>74. My 70, March 11.<anchor n="30" xml:id="d703-f1"/> The following are typical
                    examples of instructions received by the High Commissioner from Vichy.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>(1)</label>
                    <item>Although the SS <hi rendition="#italic">Providence</hi> referred to in the
                        first paragraph of my telegram No. 38, February 17, left Beirut March 1 the
                        French Government refuses to restore to the British Consul General his pouch
                        service unless the British Government agrees to a monthly sailing between
                        Beirut and Marseilles without stops at Haifa.</item>
                    <label>(2)</label>
                    <item>The High Commissioner has been instructed to facilitate in every way all
                        exports to Germany as Berlin has been complaining that the local authorities
                        have recently been showing reluctance to grant export permits for goods
                        destined for Germany. Please see in this connection my telegram 34, February
                        12, second [apparent omission] my 39 [<hi rendition="#italic">38</hi>],
                        February 17, my 40, February 18, my 63, March 7.<anchor n="31"
                            xml:id="d703-f2"/> As a result of German pressure some 50 tons of silk
                        have just been shipped to Bulgaria apparently without encountering
                        difficulties or delays in Turkey.</item>
                    <pb facs="0686" n="678" xml:id="pg678"/>
                    <label>(3)</label>
                    <item>Vichy has given orders that the defenses of Syria be immediately
                        strengthened. Numerous new earthworks and gun emplacements are being
                        prepared all along the coast and on the Palestine border which can only be
                        directed against England. There is also a rumor that Germany has demanded
                        the use of ports and landing fields (see paragraph 3 of my 49, February
                            26<anchor n="32" xml:id="d703-f3"/>). I feel that the situation will
                        soon require some such action on our part as suggested in the last paragraph
                        of my telegram 49 if it is not to deteriorate still further.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Vichy.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
